1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,453
Cocoa butter can freeze into six different
solids, and only one feels right.

2
00:00:04,453 --> 00:00:08,976
Chocolate tastes so good largely because
of how it melts and what that melt

3
00:00:08,976 --> 00:00:13,498
releases: cocoa butter, tempered into a
crystal form that melts at about 34

4
00:00:13,498 --> 00:00:18,503
degrees Celsius, stays solid in your hand
and turns liquid on your tongue, carrying

5
00:00:18,503 --> 00:00:22,181
aroma built during fermentation and
roasting up to your nose.

6
00:00:22,181 --> 00:00:27,065
The six forms were mapped with X-ray
diffraction, in work published through the

7
00:00:27,065 --> 00:00:28,981
American Oil Chemists' Society.

8
00:00:28,981 --> 00:00:33,938
But that number explains texture, not
flavour, and it says nothing about why

9
00:00:33,938 --> 00:00:34,981
anyone likes it.

10
00:00:34,981 --> 00:00:39,684
Those are separate questions, answered by
separate instruments, and one of them is

11
00:00:39,684 --> 00:00:41,003
barely answered at all.

12
00:00:41,003 --> 00:00:43,152
Why does chocolate taste so good?

13
00:00:43,152 --> 00:00:47,241
Start with the moment of eating, because
that is where the physics is easiest to

14
00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:47,445
see.

15
00:00:47,445 --> 00:00:50,928
A square of well-made dark chocolate snaps
cleanly.

16
00:00:50,928 --> 00:00:56,050
Held in a warm palm for a minute it
softens, but on the tongue it collapses

17
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:57,893
into liquid within seconds.

18
00:00:57,893 --> 00:00:59,553
That contrast is not luck.

19
00:00:59,553 --> 00:01:04,786
Cocoa butter, the fat pressed from cacao
beans, has a melting range that sits just

20
00:01:04,786 --> 00:01:07,339
under the temperature inside your mouth.

21
00:01:07,339 --> 00:01:13,085
Solid on the shelf, solid in the hand,
liquid in the mouth: a narrow window, and

22
00:01:13,085 --> 00:01:15,312
good chocolate lives inside it.

23
00:01:15,312 --> 00:01:20,469
Melting is a phase change, not a chemical
reaction, and it takes energy.

24
00:01:20,469 --> 00:01:24,943
As the fat turns from solid to liquid it
draws heat from your tongue, which is part

25
00:01:24,943 --> 00:01:28,123
of why tempered chocolate can feel faintly
cool as it goes.

26
00:01:28,123 --> 00:01:33,430
The liquid fat then coats the mouth, and
with it come sugar, cocoa solids and the

27
00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:35,789
volatile molecules that carry aroma.

28
00:01:35,789 --> 00:01:40,275
Texture comes first, and texture is the
delivery system for everything else.

29
00:01:40,275 --> 00:01:44,650
A chocolate that melts too high feels waxy
and holds its flavour back.

30
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:47,651
One that melts too low goes soft in the
wrapper.

31
00:01:47,651 --> 00:01:52,424
Here is the part that surprises people.
Cocoa butter is not one solid.

32
00:01:52,424 --> 00:01:57,483
Its fat molecules, mostly triglycerides,
can stack together in several different

33
00:01:57,483 --> 00:02:00,835
crystal arrangements, a property called
polymorphism.

34
00:02:00,835 --> 00:02:04,003
The molecules are the same; only the
packing changes.

35
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:08,607
Different packing means a different
melting point, a different hardness and a

36
00:02:08,607 --> 00:02:09,683
different surface.

37
00:02:09,683 --> 00:02:14,735
The standard scheme, published in the
Journal of the American Oil Chemists'

38
00:02:14,735 --> 00:02:19,922
Society and still the reference point in
confectionery science, describes six

39
00:02:19,922 --> 00:02:21,741
forms, numbered One to Six.

40
00:02:21,741 --> 00:02:25,271
The way chemists tell these forms apart is
X-ray diffraction.

41
00:02:25,271 --> 00:02:29,264
A beam of X-rays passed through a
crystalline sample scatters off the

42
00:02:29,264 --> 00:02:33,720
regular layers of molecules, and the
angles of the scattered beams reveal how

43
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,283
far apart those layers are.

44
00:02:35,283 --> 00:02:39,544
Each form leaves its own pattern, a kind
of fingerprint for packing.

45
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:43,718
Paired with an instrument that measures
how much heat a sample absorbs as it

46
00:02:43,718 --> 00:02:48,057
warms, known as differential scanning
calorimetry, the patterns let researchers

47
00:02:48,057 --> 00:02:51,133
match each crystal arrangement to its
melting behaviour.

48
00:02:51,133 --> 00:02:54,936
Nobody had to taste their way to this
result. It was measured.

49
00:02:54,936 --> 00:02:58,565
Of the six, Form Five is the one chocolate
makers want.

50
00:02:58,565 --> 00:03:04,042
It melts at about 34 degrees Celsius, it
is hard enough to snap, and it sets with a

51
00:03:04,042 --> 00:03:05,032
glossy surface.

52
00:03:05,032 --> 00:03:08,346
The lower forms melt too easily and feel
greasy.

53
00:03:08,346 --> 00:03:13,869
Form Six is more stable still, but it
forms slowly, over long storage, and as it

54
00:03:13,869 --> 00:03:17,389
does the surface turns dull and the
texture grainy.

55
00:03:17,389 --> 00:03:22,258
That is why the number in the cold open
matters: it is the melting point of one

56
00:03:22,258 --> 00:03:25,277
specific crystal, not of cocoa butter in
general.

57
00:03:25,277 --> 00:03:30,548
Left to cool on its own, melted chocolate
sets into a mixture of forms, and the

58
00:03:30,548 --> 00:03:32,749
result is soft, streaky and dull.

59
00:03:32,749 --> 00:03:34,123
Tempering is the fix.

60
00:03:34,123 --> 00:03:38,504
First the chocolate is heated until every
existing crystal is gone.

61
00:03:38,504 --> 00:03:41,528
Then it is cooled, so crystals begin to
form.

62
00:03:41,528 --> 00:03:46,972
Then it is warmed slightly, just enough to
melt the unstable forms while the Form

63
00:03:46,972 --> 00:03:48,451
Five crystals survive.

64
00:03:48,451 --> 00:03:53,924
Those survivors act as seeds, and as the
chocolate sets they template the rest of

65
00:03:53,924 --> 00:03:56,221
the fat into the same arrangement.

66
00:03:56,221 --> 00:04:00,867
Professionals do it on marble slabs or in
machines; the principle is the same.

67
00:04:00,867 --> 00:04:05,181
When tempering fails, or when a bar is
stored warm, you can see it.

68
00:04:05,181 --> 00:04:09,332
Grey-white streaks and a dusty film appear
on the surface.

69
00:04:09,332 --> 00:04:13,197
This is fat bloom, and it is often
mistaken for mould.

70
00:04:13,197 --> 00:04:18,397
It is fat recrystallising, frequently into
Form Six, scattering light instead of

71
00:04:18,397 --> 00:04:19,307
reflecting it.

72
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:22,522
It is a change in structure, not something
growing.

73
00:04:22,522 --> 00:04:25,360
But the melt changes, and so does the
eating.

74
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,760
The bloom is visible proof that crystal
form is part of what you taste.

75
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,417
What are people repeating without
checking?

76
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:35,568
What's hidden by the way the story is
usually told?

77
00:04:35,568 --> 00:04:40,396
The tongue detects a short list of basic
tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and

78
00:04:40,396 --> 00:04:40,885
savoury.

79
00:04:40,885 --> 00:04:45,199
Chocolate certainly registers on that
list, mostly as sweet and bitter.

80
00:04:45,199 --> 00:04:50,180
But the character people mean when they
say chocolate, the roasted, fruity, nutty,

81
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:52,307
sometimes floral quality, is aroma.

82
00:04:52,307 --> 00:04:57,473
Volatile molecules released by the melting
fat travel up the back of the throat to

83
00:04:57,473 --> 00:04:58,040
the nose.

84
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:02,641
This route is called retronasal smell, and
it is why chocolate tastes flat when your

85
00:05:02,641 --> 00:05:03,517
nose is blocked.

86
00:05:03,517 --> 00:05:07,673
You can notice it yourself by pinching
your nostrils while a piece melts, then

87
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:08,259
letting go.

88
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:12,106
So how do chemists find out which
molecules carry that aroma?

89
00:05:12,106 --> 00:05:17,215
A chocolate extract is fed through a gas
chromatograph, which separates a mixture

90
00:05:17,215 --> 00:05:22,451
into its components as they travel through
a long, thin column at different speeds.

91
00:05:22,451 --> 00:05:24,619
At the end, the stream is split.

92
00:05:24,619 --> 00:05:30,040
Part goes to a detector, often a mass
spectrometer that identifies the molecule.

93
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:35,125
Part goes to a sniffing port, where a
trained person records what each component

94
00:05:35,125 --> 00:05:36,968
smells like and how strongly.

95
00:05:36,968 --> 00:05:42,396
This is gas chromatography–olfactometry,
and the human nose is part of the

96
00:05:42,396 --> 00:05:43,203
instrument.

97
00:05:43,203 --> 00:05:47,380
Results of this kind appear regularly in
the Journal of Agricultural and Food

98
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:47,923
Chemistry.

99
00:05:47,923 --> 00:05:52,387
What this work shows is that no single
molecule smells like chocolate.

100
00:05:52,387 --> 00:05:57,515
Chemists have identified a large number of
volatile compounds in cocoa, and estimates

101
00:05:57,515 --> 00:06:01,437
of how many vary with the method and with
what counts as present.

102
00:06:01,437 --> 00:06:05,636
Only a fraction of them reach a level the
nose can detect, and the impression of

103
00:06:05,636 --> 00:06:07,683
chocolate comes from their combination.

104
00:06:07,683 --> 00:06:09,547
Remove one and the picture shifts.

105
00:06:09,547 --> 00:06:14,152
Some of the important ones, smelled alone,
would not remind you of chocolate at all.

106
00:06:14,152 --> 00:06:17,036
Fresh cacao beans taste nothing like
chocolate.

107
00:06:17,036 --> 00:06:21,453
They are bitter and astringent, packed
inside a pod in sweet white pulp.

108
00:06:21,453 --> 00:06:26,696
After harvest, beans and pulp are heaped
or boxed and left for several days.

109
00:06:26,696 --> 00:06:31,223
Yeasts ferment the sugars in the pulp,
then bacteria turn the products into

110
00:06:31,223 --> 00:06:32,611
lactic and acetic acid.

111
00:06:32,611 --> 00:06:37,715
Heat builds, the acids enter the bean, and
the bean's internal structure breaks down.

112
00:06:37,715 --> 00:06:43,160
Its own enzymes begin cutting proteins
into smaller pieces and splitting sugars.

113
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:47,646
Those fragments are flavour precursors:
they do not yet smell of chocolate, but

114
00:06:47,646 --> 00:06:50,259
without them roasting has little to work
with.

115
00:06:50,259 --> 00:06:52,925
Roasting is where the precursors pay off.

116
00:06:52,925 --> 00:06:58,324
Heated together, amino acids and reducing
sugars undergo the Maillard reaction, the

117
00:06:58,324 --> 00:07:02,552
same family of reactions that browns bread
crust and seared meat.

118
00:07:02,552 --> 00:07:07,981
It produces a cascade of new molecules,
among them pyrazines, which carry roasted

119
00:07:07,981 --> 00:07:09,053
and nutty notes.

120
00:07:09,053 --> 00:07:13,945
Researchers established the role of
fermentation partly by comparison: beans

121
00:07:13,945 --> 00:07:19,416
dried without proper fermentation and then
roasted develop only weak chocolate aroma.

122
00:07:19,416 --> 00:07:24,139
The farm, in other words, writes much of
the recipe before the beans ever reach a

123
00:07:24,139 --> 00:07:24,605
factory.

124
00:07:24,605 --> 00:07:29,582
After roasting, the beans are cracked, the
shells removed, and the nibs ground into a

125
00:07:29,582 --> 00:07:29,933
paste.

126
00:07:29,933 --> 00:07:34,411
Sugar and often extra cocoa butter are
added, the mixture is ground until the

127
00:07:34,411 --> 00:07:39,063
particles are too fine for the tongue to
notice as grit, and then it is conched:

128
00:07:39,063 --> 00:07:40,808
stirred and kneaded for hours.

129
00:07:40,808 --> 00:07:46,536
Conching lets some harsh, volatile acids
escape and coats every solid particle in

130
00:07:46,536 --> 00:07:46,819
fat.

131
00:07:46,819 --> 00:07:50,232
That coating is why smooth chocolate feels
smooth.

132
00:07:50,232 --> 00:07:54,397
Once again, texture and flavour turn out
not to be separable.

133
00:07:54,397 --> 00:07:56,165
Chocolate is not only sweet.

134
00:07:56,165 --> 00:08:00,837
Cacao contains theobromine, a bitter
compound closely related to caffeine,

135
00:08:00,837 --> 00:08:06,013
along with polyphenols that taste bitter
and feel astringent, a drying pull on the

136
00:08:06,013 --> 00:08:06,392
mouth.

137
00:08:06,392 --> 00:08:10,910
Theobromine takes its name from the genus
of the cacao tree, Theobroma, from the

138
00:08:10,910 --> 00:08:12,435
Greek for food of the gods.

139
00:08:12,435 --> 00:08:16,817
Its structure and properties are
catalogued in PubChem, the open chemistry

140
00:08:16,817 --> 00:08:19,779
database run by the National Institutes of
Health.

141
00:08:19,779 --> 00:08:22,328
Bitterness here is not a flaw to be
removed.

142
00:08:22,328 --> 00:08:27,137
Makers balance it against sugar and fat,
and that balance is much of what separates

143
00:08:27,137 --> 00:08:28,701
a dark bar from a milk one.

144
00:08:28,701 --> 00:08:32,565
This is also why preference splits so
sharply between people.

145
00:08:32,565 --> 00:08:37,822
The same chemistry reaches every tongue,
but sensitivity to bitterness differs from

146
00:08:37,822 --> 00:08:41,496
person to person, and so does what someone
grew up eating.

147
00:08:41,496 --> 00:08:45,523
A bar one person finds richly complex,
another finds harsh.

148
00:08:45,523 --> 00:08:48,527
Food science can describe the bar
precisely.

149
00:08:48,527 --> 00:08:52,008
It cannot, from the bar alone, predict the
verdict.

150
00:08:52,008 --> 00:08:54,813
Now to what people repeat without
checking.

151
00:08:54,813 --> 00:09:00,356
A popular story says chocolate tastes good
because it contains special molecules that

152
00:09:00,356 --> 00:09:03,683
act on the brain, sometimes called a love
chemical.

153
00:09:03,683 --> 00:09:08,802
Some of the compounds named in that story
are genuinely present in cocoa; that part

154
00:09:08,802 --> 00:09:09,480
checks out.

155
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:13,756
What has not been shown is that they reach
the brain from an ordinary piece of

156
00:09:13,756 --> 00:09:16,333
chocolate in amounts that explain the
pleasure.

157
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:20,402
How much liking is learned rather than
triggered remains open.

158
00:09:20,402 --> 00:09:25,718
The story survives because it is simpler
than the real one, which is a crystal, a

159
00:09:25,718 --> 00:09:28,605
melt, a nose and a long chain of
processing.

160
00:09:28,605 --> 00:09:31,598
Notice what the stronger explanation rests
on.

161
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:37,064
Crystal forms are measured by diffraction
and calorimetry, in work published through

162
00:09:37,064 --> 00:09:39,341
the American Oil Chemists' Society.

163
00:09:39,341 --> 00:09:43,952
Aroma compounds are separated by
chromatography and confirmed by trained

164
00:09:43,952 --> 00:09:48,499
noses, in journals like the Journal of
Agricultural and Food Chemistry.

165
00:09:48,499 --> 00:09:51,470
The bitter compounds are catalogued in
PubChem.

166
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:56,717
Each step has an instrument behind it, and
each step can be checked by someone else

167
00:09:56,717 --> 00:09:57,539
repeating it.

168
00:09:57,539 --> 00:10:01,016
The pleasure molecule story has none of
that chain.

169
00:10:01,016 --> 00:10:04,221
It has a plausible name and a satisfying
shape.

170
00:10:04,221 --> 00:10:08,810
When the cross-section of tempered
chocolate appears, watch the packing, not

171
00:10:08,810 --> 00:10:09,475
the colour.

172
00:10:09,475 --> 00:10:14,472
The glow marks the Form Five crystals, the
ordered layers that diffraction measures.

173
00:10:14,472 --> 00:10:20,354
The animation is a model, not a microscope
image; real crystals are far smaller and

174
00:10:20,354 --> 00:10:21,347
far less tidy.

175
00:10:21,347 --> 00:10:25,935
The degree counter earlier showed one
temperature only, about 34 degrees

176
00:10:25,935 --> 00:10:30,013
Celsius, because that is the one figure
this episode can source.

177
00:10:30,013 --> 00:10:34,648
Everything else, from roasting heat to
conching time, varies with the maker, and

178
00:10:34,648 --> 00:10:36,792
the graphics leave it out on purpose.

179
00:10:36,792 --> 00:10:39,892
Be clear about what this evidence does not
establish.

180
00:10:39,892 --> 00:10:44,804
It explains why chocolate melts the way it
does, where much of its aroma comes from,

181
00:10:44,804 --> 00:10:47,027
and why processing changes the result.

182
00:10:47,027 --> 00:10:51,311
It does not explain why a particular
person loves it, or why liking differs

183
00:10:51,311 --> 00:10:52,739
across people and places.

184
00:10:52,739 --> 00:10:57,847
Those are questions about perception,
memory and culture, and the instruments in

185
00:10:57,847 --> 00:10:59,955
this episode do not measure them.

186
00:10:59,955 --> 00:11:05,005
Nothing here says anything about chocolate
and health; that is a different body of

187
00:11:05,005 --> 00:11:07,715
evidence, and not one this episode
examined.

188
00:11:07,715 --> 00:11:11,247
So the next square you eat is doing
something precise.

189
00:11:11,247 --> 00:11:16,612
A crystal chosen by tempering gives way
just below the heat of your mouth, and the

190
00:11:16,612 --> 00:11:21,911
fat releases molecules built by microbes
and a roaster, long before and far away.

191
00:11:21,911 --> 00:11:25,901
The pleasure is yours. The melt, at least,
has been measured.
